\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Correlation Matrix of the \textit  {MLE} fit for the set of data at \SI {1.03}{\tesla }. The block of parameters identified as $xZ_xx_ry$ ($xx$ denote the transition type, $y$ the transition number) corresponds to the frequency markers (the nomenclature used in the analysis code). It's evident the correlation of the frequency markers within the same transition, as their correlation with the shape parameter $\sigma _{xx}$. Correlations are also observed among others shape parameters $\sigma _{R,xx}$ and $n_{r,xx}$. These do not posa a problem since they influence the depletion tail of spectrum, and are insensitive to onset position.}}{42}{figure.4.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:CorrelationHeatMap}{{4.5}{42}{Correlation Matrix of the \textit {MLE} fit for the set of data at \SI {1.03}{\tesla }. The block of parameters identified as $xZ_xx_ry$ ($xx$ denote the transition type, $y$ the transition number) corresponds to the frequency markers (the nomenclature used in the analysis code). It's evident the correlation of the frequency markers within the same transition, as their correlation with the shape parameter $\sigma _{xx}$. Correlations are also observed among others shape parameters $\sigma _{R,xx}$ and $n_{r,xx}$. These do not posa a problem since they influence the depletion tail of spectrum, and are insensitive to onset position}{figure.4.5}{}}
