\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces This plots shows the results from the analysis of the 2023 data. This analysis has been used to constrain magnetic upward drift induced from beam-line magnet manipulation during antihydrogen stacking phase. The change of the beam-line magnets during stacking can induce parasitic currents on Mirror and Solenoid magnets, causing a small magnetic upward drift. Since the \textbf  {da} transitions always follows the \textbf  {cb} transition, this effect can generate a systematic shift in the frequency that change the value of the splitting. This shift has been constrained using 2023 data that were collected with two protocols. In the first protocol there are \SI {32}{\second } between the start of the microwave measurement. In the second protocol the time interval is increased to \SI {352}{\second }. With this setup, the \textbf  {cb} transition with the \SI {352}{\second } delay starts at the same time, with respect to the beam-line magnet manipulation, of the \textbf  {da} transition with the \SI {32}{\second } delay. The frequency markers shown are the result of the analysis on Pass Cuts data.}}{57}{figure.4.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ShortUpwardBdrift}{{4.10}{57}{This plots shows the results from the analysis of the 2023 data. This analysis has been used to constrain magnetic upward drift induced from beam-line magnet manipulation during antihydrogen stacking phase. The change of the beam-line magnets during stacking can induce parasitic currents on Mirror and Solenoid magnets, causing a small magnetic upward drift. Since the \da transitions always follows the \cb transition, this effect can generate a systematic shift in the frequency that change the value of the splitting. This shift has been constrained using 2023 data that were collected with two protocols. In the first protocol there are \SI {32}{\second } between the start of the microwave measurement. In the second protocol the time interval is increased to \SI {352}{\second }. With this setup, the \cb transition with the \SI {352}{\second } delay starts at the same time, with respect to the beam-line magnet manipulation, of the \da transition with the \SI {32}{\second } delay. The frequency markers shown are the result of the analysis on Pass Cuts data}{figure.4.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Interpolation of the $cb$ frequency markers (points) with Gaussian Process Regression for Series 7 (left) and Series 8 (right). The linear trend is removed before interpolation (detrending is such that the ordinates of the first and last points are zero). The abscissa is the time of the repetition in seconds and the ordinate is the residual w.r.t. the linear trend of the frequency markers, in kHz. The band represents the $95\%$ confidence interval. Top and bottom figures are obtained with different settings for the input noise, without significant changes in the estimate of the systematic uncertainty. The top plots correspond to the settings used for the estimate in Tab.~\ref {tab:syst}}}{58}{figure.4.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:GPR}{{4.11}{58}{Interpolation of the $cb$ frequency markers (points) with Gaussian Process Regression for Series 7 (left) and Series 8 (right). The linear trend is removed before interpolation (detrending is such that the ordinates of the first and last points are zero). The abscissa is the time of the repetition in seconds and the ordinate is the residual w.r.t. the linear trend of the frequency markers, in kHz. The band represents the $95\%$ confidence interval. Top and bottom figures are obtained with different settings for the input noise, without significant changes in the estimate of the systematic uncertainty. The top plots correspond to the settings used for the estimate in Tab.~\ref {tab:syst}}{figure.4.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Example of DCCTs traces for a total of 40 second of acquisition. The units on the y-axis are Volts, since the DCCT provides a $\pm 10 $ V output. The traces have been converted in Ampere units and the amplitude of the glitches has been used to study possible discrepancies on the measurement induced by change in the magnetic field minimum }}{59}{figure.4.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:DCCTsTraces}{{4.12}{59}{Example of DCCTs traces for a total of 40 second of acquisition. The units on the y-axis are Volts, since the DCCT provides a $\pm 10 $ V output. The traces have been converted in Ampere units and the amplitude of the glitches has been used to study possible discrepancies on the measurement induced by change in the magnetic field minimum}{figure.4.12}{}}
